 number of drawbacks and that  is the reason for the  growing concern about  how we can improve the quality of life  of individuals and how we can prevent  the disease or if not preventable at  least treat them and  improve the quality of life of cancer  patients  the last 20 years from the year 2000  has seen a large number of small  molecules that are coming up for  targeted cancer therapy there are a  number of targets  that have been explored and small  molecules that target these  receptors  or pathways in  for cancer  have shown a lot of promise however  if we look  in terms of the experience of patients  we don't see much  improvement and according to the world  world health organization  every year in india at least 10  10 lakh individuals  suffer from the disease and the spending  in a cancer  suffering  household is at least 36 to 44  higher than as compared to households of  similar demographics even the cost of  treatment has gone up so what is really  wrong are we not having drugs or if you  are having drugs then why are we not  able to reduce the cost and what is the  way to improve the quality of life of  individuals who  suffer from the disease so this is uh  something that  that needs to be looked into  so all this has given  initially there was the focus was on  traditional medicine which included  radiation chemotherapy and surgery but  now this has given way to more targeted  therapies the involvement of genetics of  individuals and also immunotherapy  for treatment of the disease  [Music]  so also our group  at amity is involved in study of the  folate receptor and we are looking into  new drug delivery options uh how we can  deliver low concentration of the drug to  the cancer cells in a targeted way and  the folate receptor appears as a very  attractive target  for cancer drug delivery the reason  being that first of all it has been  newly identified and  there are three isoforms of the folate  receptor the folate receptor alpha beta  and gamma however the folate receptor  alpha  has uh has seen a lot of success and  promising therapeutic target in a  variety of cancers  non-malignant tissues  express very limited amount of folate  receptor alpha whereas  the  the  malignant tissues and especially solid  tumors uh the ovarian cancer kidney lung  brain  breast cancer  lung cancer and you name it all these  have  over expression of um the folate  receptor  also what is important is that folate  the folate receptor alpha has high  affinity for folic acid and conjugates  of folic acid can be considered as good  um  opportunities to target the cancer cells  and a number of clinical trials are also  ongoing in this area  so  i will spend some time um talking about  the accessibility of the cellular uptake  through the folate receptor we know that  80 percent of the human cancers are  derived from epithelial cells and those  receptors that are restricted to the  apical regions of the cells  can be considered as good targets  because  when drugs are administered parentally  they are not  accessible to normal cells and this is a  big advantage because most of the drugs  that were already available they lack  selectivity especially the  chemotherapeutic drugs they lack  selectivity they show a lot of toxicity  because they not only target  the cancer cells they also target the  normal cells so if you are using a  targeted approach then obviously you  will be able to  take care of  the off target toxicity which is seen in  most cases  so um since receptors are epically  expressed so when uh the drug is  administered verily it is not accessible  to the norm to the normal cells however  when the cells become malignant and the  tight junctions collapse in those cases  the folate receptor becomes  redistributed  on the cell membrane and that is the  reason why it becomes accessible to um  drug uh conjugates which may be uh  administered parentally so this is a big  advantage that uh where you can opt for  targeted drug delivery  so uh three general  strategies have been uh explored uh we  have anti-folate receptor antibody  approach the humanized folate receptor  alpha  antibody drug conjugate approach and the  folic acid smdc approach smdc is the  small molecule drug conjugate approach  so if you look at the molecule of folic  acid  it comprises of three regions you have  the terridine ring you have the para  aminobenzoic  acid  portion of the molecule and you have the  glutamic acid  so it is the pterydine ring which is  primarily involved in the binding to the  folate receptor therefore this part of  the molecule is very much available for  functionalization and can be used  for drug delivery  so a couple of drugs just as examples  so here we have the antibody approach uh  where phallitis map has been  has been tested not only in phase two  but also in phase three and  what has been seen so far is that it did  not meet the primary end point of  improving progression-free survival in  patients  we  there are also some studies which show  the antibody drug conjugate approach and  mirv is one such molecule  one such agent which has been tested  and it is showing some promise but uh  still in phase one and phase two  then we have the folic acid small  molecule drug conjugate approach so full  uh the folic acid conjugates can be used  to deliver the drug payload to the  cancer cells so uh  so what what it  um involves is that folic acid can be  conjugated using um  cleavable bonds and these can carry the  drug payload to the cancer cell once  this  once the folic acid binds to the  receptor it is internalized and the  internal ph of the endosome will result  in the cleavage of the folic acid from  its receptor and also release the drug  payload now once the drug payload is  released the folate receptor recycles  back to the cell membrane and the drug  is  is  delivered at low concentration to the  cancer cells  so that is a very uh interesting  approach and our work is in that  direction however we have made some  changes which i will be talking about  so winter folied is one such molecule  where they have uh tested a vinca  alkaloid and they have tried to  deliver it to cancer cells this molecule  showed a lot of promise  however it is now out of phase 3 trials  so keeping all this  in mind  we looked into the literature and  curcumin which is a well-known  spice which is used in indian food and  it has a lot of medicinal property  we looked into this molecule it's a  wonder molecule because it has  a whole range of  activity  and shows very a lot of promise but  there are some things which have to be  taken care of so it has been associated  with antioxidant anti-inflammatory  anti-cancer antiviral antibacterial  properties  it is also known to chemosensitize many  chemotherapeutic agents such as  5-fluorouracil doxorivisin paclitaxels  platinum etc  the only  drawback of this molecule is its poor  bioavailability and poor absorption so  that is what limits its use  in the clinical setting  so  keeping in mind the multiple targets  that this molecule  is known to modulate  we have designed our own  small molecule drug conjugate  which comprises of folic acid  there is a spacer unit which connects it  to curcumin and finally there is a  cleavable bond  that is  connecting to an anti-cancer drug and we  have used paclitaxel we have used  acid sensitive  cleavable bonds which can cleave at in  the acidic ph of  of the endosome  so i'm going to share some of the  results that preliminary results that  we've got with this triple conjugate  that we have designed uh we have uh  tested our conjugate in  the uh cervical cancers cancer cell  lines like hela and seahaw cell lines  and what we find when we do the mtt  assay of these compounds uh is that our  conjugate is uh working as good as um  the known drug doctor of the sin which  is which is really important because uh  curcumin might be assisting in its um  in um in the drug entering the cancer  cell  the cell proliferation assay also um  when we compare it with curcumin  toxorovicin and with untreated cells we  see that it is showing comparable  activity in at 12 and 24 hours both in  heli and see her cell lines  in order to look at uh  its  ability to enter the cells because it's  a large molecule  is it able to really enter the cells so  we have carried out these  studies where we have seen that our  molecule is able to  enter the cells and this green  fluorescence that you are seeing is of  curcumin which has entered the cancer  cells  after treatment  we have seen it at six hours and 18  hours  using not only curcumin but also  the conjugate and our conjugate  exhibited better  fluorescence as compared to curcumin  which is evident from the figures  we also carried out some  in-vitro cell migration assay using our  conjugate uh doxorubicin and curcumin  and with the controls and what we found  is that in case of the con  the untreated cells uh whereas there was  100 migration of cells but when uh when  we treated with  either of the agents we could see that  cell migration was very much inhibited  um  by using the conjugate specifically  so we've also compared the apoptosis of  our  we have carried out the apoptosis assay  with our conjugate curcumin and oxybusin  in the same cell lines and we find that  this molecule this conjugate in fact uh  shows uh the cells are  more apoptotic  when we treat them with the triple  conjugate  so just to summarize uh  what we have  got so far  is that a conjugate  is uh exhibiting better cytotoxic  activity as compared to curcumin or  oxalism alone uh there is a 30 to 70  percent reduction in cell proliferation  in both hela and csl lines  in our uptick experiment we did see that  the  conjugate could  internalize in the cell and we have seen  it visually that  more concentration of the conjugate or  curcumin which is  could be seen inside the cell which  means that  indeed the conjugate was internalized  inside the cell  the scratch assay  again  showed significant reduction in the  migration rate  as compared to the untreated controls  and the apoptosis assay after treatment  with curcumin doxorivisin um showed that  the apoptosis rate was uh much higher  when  when we  used our conjugate so all these studies  gave us a lot of confidence and now we  are proceeding ahead with more in bible  studies  for our  conjugate and we believe that curcumin  would be  the game changer for this  for this conjugate because it it is  assisting  in the entry of the drug by  chemosensitizing the cell of course most  studies are required for it but  our preliminary results uh indicate that  probably curcumin is assisting and the  drug is able to enter the cell at low  concentration so if uh it is able to  enter at low concentration uh we are  able to also target the cell so we  expect that the off-target toxicity will  be taken care of and at low  concentration we are expecting similar  results as compared to the uh known drug  so um my next part of my presentation is  about the graduate and the post graduate  programs at at my department so we are  we are amity institute of biotechnology  at amity university  and we offer a lot of programs in the  area of biotechnology which includes  b-tech b-tech bioinformatics bsc  programs in biotechnology  bscmsc dual btec dual three continent  programs  and  we also offer bsc biotechnology and b8  courses  and  for our postgraduate programs also we  are offering  the mba biotechnology program msc by  biotechnology bioinformatics mtech  biotechnology and of course our phd  programs we also have some certificate  programs in artificial intelligence we  have masters of technology in  bioinformatics  so it's a big focus in our department  and we have  a large number of students who are  really interested  in all aspects of biotechnology  our institution also offers many uh  opportunities for international  opportunities for students which  include the student exchange programs  the which are offered in france where  students can spend a semester abroad  and complete their studies in a foreign  university we offer study abroad  programs at four or five locations all  over the world and this these are  available to all students ug and pg  students at amity institute of  biotechnology  we also have  a very unique three continent program  now this program  we have  three streams in which we run them the  btec biotech three continent bsc medical  biotech three continent and the bse  honors biotech three continent so in  this the students  complete most of their studies in india  but in the last semester they travel to  uk and u.s for three months each and  finally they get their degree from amity  university  we are also collaborating with many  universities abroad and we are offering  international collaborative programs in  b tech bioinformatics btec biotechnology  international again it is a two plus two  program uh with indiana university and  the bsc honors biotechnology program is  with the university of queensland  australia it's a oneplus 2 program so  students have a plethora of  opportunities if they join the  department and  truly  we believe in creating global citizens  at amity and our students have always  made us proud um  so  and we hope to continue the same in  future  so thank you very much uh thank you for  your patient hearing if there are any  questions i am ready to answer them  thank you very much for this  presentation we do have a few brief  questions here um one of them are  are there any specific techniques  required to synthesize these kinds of  molecules uh he means conjugate  yes so uh conjugation chemistry would  all uh  involve chemical synthesis of the  conjugates but yes it's very important  to  uh characterize whether conjugation  really took place or not so  of course you would be using ir nmr and  you have to use since it's a large  molecule so you may have to  go for malditov to  know the molecular weight of the  molecule so all that is very important  because  just carrying out the synthesis does not  mean that the conjugate got made you  have to characterize it

